  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 740 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nThis evening we're watching a cold front moving into our area\nbringing potential for strong to severe storms, mainly in NE\nNebraska. Looking at satellite, we've been watching storms go up\nin eastern SD with growing cu trailing south along the frontal\nboundary toward O'Neill. Cu has struggled south of Hutchinson \nCounty so far, likely due to more dry air they need to overcome \nfarther south from the surface low. We do note cu development \nin Boyd and NE Holt counties, though. If any of them can get \ngoing, expect a fairly solid line of storms to light up as it \nmoves into NE Nebraska. Still only a 20-40% chance of storms \nthough. \n\nIf storms do develop, the environment will support a few\nstronger storms with potential for 50-60+ mph winds and up to\nquarter-size hail. As they continue southeast, though, they will\nonly encounter more and more dry air with a strengthening warm\nnose aloft. What will more likely occur is if a line of storms\ndevelop, they go up around western Knox/Antelope Counties, peak\njust east near Norfolk to Albion before falling apart as they\nmove through Columbus-Wisner. \n\nWinds shift to northerly behind the front bringing in cooler air\nfor the weekend. We'll see another shortwave trigger storms on\nan elevated boundary along the leading edge of the Canadian \nHigh Saturday mid-morning, continuing southeast through SE \nNebraska and SW Iowa through the afternoon. Coverage of storms \nwill greatly depend on the amount of elevated instability the \nenvironment can produce with warmer air in place in the lower \nlevels and cooling temperatures aloft as the High moves in. If \nwe hold onto cloud cover into Saturday, this may help to hold \nwarmer air in the low levels increasing elevated instability, \nwhile if we clear out we may not get the needed steep lapse \nrates. Bulk shear (0-6km) of 45 to 55kt could support some \nbowing convective clusters with potential for damaging winds and\nlarge hail. With bases up around 9000 ft there is almost no \ntornado threat. High pressure remains in place through the rest \nof the evening clearing skies behind the storms. \n\nThe active pattern continues as another shortwave develops a\nsurface low over the Texas Panhandle pushing the remnant frontal\nboundary (from today) back north of the kansas state line\nSaturday night into early Sunday. This brings showers and\nstorms back to southeast Nebraska/southwest Iowa overnight into\nSunday ahead of and along the advancing warm front. Expect\nSunday to be a fairly rainy day with a transition from more\nstratiform rain overnight with increasing convective \ndevelopment Sunday morning. \n\nWe see a more substantial warm conveyor belt develop across the\nPlains from Oklahoma to Manitoba Sunday night into Monday ahead\nof a stronger upper-level trough moving across central Canada \nand the northern Plains. This will wrap into a developing \nsurface tracking from eastern Colorado into south-central \nNebraska on Monday. This is a good set up for enhanced low-level\nshear and instability, potentially bringing a fairly decent \nall-hazards severe weather threat Monday evening to eastern \nNebraska and much of Iowa. \n\nGoing into next week, the active pattern appears likely to\ncontinue with southwesterly flow bringing a clash of\nsoutherly flow advecting moisture into the region while cooler\nair keeps trying to push south along a series of shortwave\ntroughs moving through. This will continue to bring periodic\nchances for showers and storms with fairly seasonable\ntemperatures.",
